WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.700
GitHub, branching, pull requests and rebasing.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.389
Git stores your history.

3
00:00:05.389 --> 00:00:08.224
GitHub is where a team, or a recruiter, reads it.

4
00:00:08.224 --> 00:00:10.133
Let's see the habits that matter.

5
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:14.140
GitHub is the collaboration layer on top of Git.

6
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.700
You work on a feature branch, and a pull request shows the diff to a reviewer.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.967
Branch protection makes review and green checks mandatory on main.

8
00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:27.633
And a squash merge collapses all the fix-up noise into one clean commit.

9
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:30.567
Here is the life of one change.

10
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:32.900
You isolate the work on its own branch.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.800
You open a pull request with a clear description.

12
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.900
A reviewer reads it, and you answer every comment.

13
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.500
CI runs, and nothing broken can land, mechanically.

14
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.533
Finally, you squash merge, and one clean commit reaches main.

15
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.933
Hiring managers really do read your pull requests.

16
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.967
A good PR has one concern.

17
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:56.100
A two hundred line diff gets reviewed in hours.

18
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.100
A giant two thousand line one sits for a week.

19
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.133
State what changed, why, and how you tested it.

20
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:05.100
Small and clear is the habit that gets merged.

21
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.033
Here is that loop in the terminal.

22
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:10.233
We create a feature branch and push it.

23
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:15.900
Then we open the pull request, with a title and a description covering the speed-up and rollback.

24
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.600
We check that CI is green, and squash merge into main.

25
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.267
Here is why it pays off.

26
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:26.867
A hiring manager spends ninety seconds on your repo before the interview.

27
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:34.400
They see a protected main, where every change arrived as a small pull request with a crisp description and green checks.

28
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.867
That reads as real team experience, and it costs about five extra minutes per change.

29
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:41.833
Let's recap.

30
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:44.067
Feature branches isolate your work.

31
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:46.833
Pull requests put the diff in front of a reviewer.

32
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:50.233
Branch protection with CI makes the checks mandatory.

33
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:54.224
And small diffs with clear descriptions show seniority.

34
00:01:54.224 --> 00:01:55.767
Next up: Docker basics.
